-- The grammar curriculum, reviewed.
--
-- Three problems with the map as first seeded, all of which a learner walking it would hit:
--
--   * Core topics were missing. There was no "to be" — the first thing anyone learns — and no
--     there is/are, have got, imperatives, used to, past simple vs present perfect, wish,
--     modals of deduction, and almost nothing at C1/C2 (participle clauses, cleft sentences,
--     ellipsis, nominalisation, the subjunctive). 36 topics are added, as drafts: a topic
--     goes live when its content is published, never before.
--   * Topics were listed twice. Comparative and superlative under Adjectives and Comparisons,
--     adjective order and adverb position under Word Order as well, a whole Relative Clauses
--     and Conditionals topic again under Clauses, "state/action verbs" beside "stative
--     verbs", "unless" in two categories, a few / a little apart from few / little. 13 are
--     archived (never deleted: progress on them stays readable). One that is already live is
--     left alone — taking published teaching away belongs to an editor, not a migration.
--   * The order was not a learning order. Within a category, B1 topics came before A2 ones
--     (Present Perfect before Past Simple, May/Might before Must), and the categories began
--     with articles rather than the verbs a beginner meets first. Categories now run from
--     what a beginner needs to what an advanced learner refines, and topics within each run
--     by level.
--
-- curriculum.json in cmd/seed is the same curriculum for a fresh database. Names and
-- descriptions an owner may have edited are only written for the topics added or renamed
-- here; for the rest only placement changes. On an empty database every statement is a
-- no-op: the seed writes the curriculum there.

UPDATE grammar_categories c SET sort_order = v.ord
FROM (VALUES
    ('verbs', 1),
    ('pronouns', 2),
    ('articles', 3),
    ('nouns', 4),
    ('determiners', 5),
    ('tenses', 6),
    ('questions-negation', 7),
    ('prepositions', 8),
    ('adjectives', 9),
    ('adverbs', 10),
    ('comparisons', 11),
    ('modals', 12),
    ('conjunctions', 13),
    ('word-order', 14),
    ('sentence-structure', 15),
    ('gerunds-infinitives', 16),
    ('conditionals', 17),
    ('passive', 18),
    ('reported-speech', 19),
    ('relative-clauses', 20),
    ('clauses', 21)
) AS v(slug, ord)
WHERE c.slug = v.slug;

